.TH SDL_snprintf 3 "SDL 3.5.0" "Simple Directmedia Layer" "SDL3 FUNCTIONS"
.SH NAME
SDL_snprintf \- This works exactly like snprintf() but doesn\(aqt require access to a C runtime.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <SDL3/SDL_stdinc.h>
.PP
.BI "int SDL_snprintf(char *text, size_t maxlen, const char *fmt, ...);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Format a string of up to
.BR maxlen -1
bytes, converting each \(aq%\(aq item with values provided through variable arguments\[char46]
.PP
While some C runtimes differ on how to deal with too-large strings, this function null-terminates the output, by treating the null-terminator as part of the
.BR maxlen
count\[char46] Note that if
.BR maxlen
is zero, however, no bytes will be written at all\[char46]
.PP
This function returns the number of _bytes_ (not _characters_) that should be written, excluding the null-terminator character\[char46] If this returns a number >=
.BR maxlen ,
it means the output string was truncated\[char46] A negative return value means an error occurred\[char46]
.PP
Referencing the output string\(aqs pointer with a format item is undefined behavior\[char46]

.SH FUNCTION PARAMETERS
.TP
.I text
the buffer to write the string into\[char46] Must not be NULL\[char46]
.TP
.I maxlen
the maximum bytes to write, including the null-terminator\[char46]
.TP
.I fmt
a printf-style format string\[char46] Must not be NULL\[char46]
.TP
.I ...
a list of values to be used with the format string\[char46]
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns the number of bytes that should be written, not counting the null-terminator char, or a negative value on error\[char46]

.SH THREAD SAFETY
It is safe to call this function from any thread\[char46]

.SH AVAILABILITY
This function is available since SDL 3\[char46]2\[char46]0\[char46]

